WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Welcome to the course on LLM evaluations. Today we are discussing why evaluation is critical for production AI systems.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Have you ever evaluated your LLM application properly? Mostly, what developers do is build an LLM-based application, ask 4 or 5 questions that come to mind, and if the answers look good, assume it is working.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:50.000
In the industry, this is called vibe testing. Vibe testing means casually trying an LLM application with a few prompts and judging it by feel. It is informal, subjective, and not repeatable.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Vibe testing works only at the personal project level. You cannot make production grade software by doing vibe testing because changes to prompts, models, or retrieval will break things unpredictably.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Instead, we need systematic evaluation. Offline eval means evaluating your system on a curated dataset of golden test cases before deployment. You have known queries and expected ground truth or ideal contexts.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Online eval means evaluating your system on live production traffic after deployment. It works without an answer key, unlike offline eval, by monitoring user feedback, latency, costs, and automated judge metrics.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Now what is drift and why does it matter? After deployment, real world user behavior changes, topics shift, or external dependencies update. When the incoming queries drift away from what you tested offline, system performance degrades.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Regression testing ensures that when you improve your prompt, update your vector database, or swap models, you do not break previously working answers. A regression suite runs your goldens and gates releases on quality, safety, and operational guardrails.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:03:00.000
In RAG pipelines, we evaluate the RAG triad: Contextual Relevancy, Faithfulness, and Answer Relevancy. Faithfulness measures whether the claims in the generated response are grounded purely in the retrieved context without hallucination.
